#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae552dad-8e26-5c41-a2b9-d8d9937ed6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387002} {:answer/id #uuid "a9733419-8d21-5608-8f7b-93f4e793048a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387003} {:answer/id #uuid "c257cba8-19ab-5321-a41a-f812052cea8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387004} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fd5867-0df2-518c-9728-b3678f36a31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387005} {:answer/id #uuid "ab76e0bd-e39f-5d1d-bd5f-d94612f118c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae552dad-8e26-5c41-a2b9-d8d9937ed6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387002} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d3c4c6-0b44-5b0d-a6f9-1147ba188c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97809c67-1610-5a64-b7c8-7cd83b34b3da" :question/math-academy-id "q-132122" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b101f016-6b03-560e-9ceb-962053a6bd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,3⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387009} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6643cd-7936-5c30-8c43-251437a3c93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387010} {:answer/id #uuid "7213c232-9aa2-5f34-af0b-314d43e800a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,2⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387011} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2a82f7-d40b-5359-8629-740cc96c9be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨2,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387012} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ecf28d-3816-5163-a87a-b9ebf7ff3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6643cd-7936-5c30-8c43-251437a3c93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387010} :answer-field/id #uuid "e92735a5-d274-5884-b5a8-b12918f8a888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daba19b1-aa22-55bb-bccd-e7fd5bf20ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132208" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e494894f-26ab-5a15-8568-2aefce607e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1, - π)$ and the radius is $π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118198} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c2eb6b-507d-59c1-abe5-25fd0b40a55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1, 0)$ and the radius is $π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118199} {:answer/id #uuid "069da814-8009-58f0-8560-9c419235f6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,-2π)$ and the radius is $π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118200} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6cdd00-0d71-51b1-b88f-5c4195f4baa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,-2π)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118201} {:answer/id #uuid "24966470-4805-528b-a523-619489213c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1, 0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c2eb6b-507d-59c1-abe5-25fd0b40a55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1, 0)$ and the radius is $π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118199} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03b5fc7-ccde-5f1d-a9ae-b59f67639897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b843aaf-f0c5-5942-abb0-c6ab73ca4229" :question/math-academy-id "q-34624" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩$,  since the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\pi }{1}=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩+\\pi ⟨0,1⟩=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453c152f-4a39-589b-9664-bbecc1420b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387016} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb32247-27d4-585b-aba1-65892697e304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387017} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6217eb-aeae-5dc4-9283-bc3784b38a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} {:answer/id #uuid "2823a55d-4cdc-5823-a52d-48671a5a7e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387019} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e3e10e-ed8f-5d64-976b-2a4870da7642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b6217eb-aeae-5dc4-9283-bc3784b38a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} :answer-field/id #uuid "64aa0e09-0dd6-52a7-a88c-ddc291c107be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "196994ed-bfc3-5fed-9e4d-698428010c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98665" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61075f5a-cfa8-52b5-ae39-54cfcd0a0af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387023} {:answer/id #uuid "20e52fdb-c6d7-5115-92f5-c446aee04465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387024} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3bfbd3-2664-5a22-9a70-13164ea1f366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387025} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9e8817-aa22-5025-be5d-ac78f9355643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387026} {:answer/id #uuid "926154dc-59d7-5bd4-ab81-78f2e4a75eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9e8817-aa22-5025-be5d-ac78f9355643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387026} :answer-field/id #uuid "796a22b1-3090-584f-9b8f-6ee3ef952863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce8b4ff6-eb45-5828-b653-cf9af8712cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-98708" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
